analysis:
Examined base text of scripts/test-provider-ci-workflow.py (lines 1-799), an offline unittest that asserts the CI workflow structure. The head adds a .git cache mechanism (head 155-187), which introduces CHECKOUT/CACHE_RESTORE/CACHE_SAVE pinned SHAs, a SAVE_POLICY restricting cache saves to push events on master/dev, and VALIDATE_GIT (git rev-parse plus fsck --connectivity-only, with rm -rf .git on failure). It also adds sparse patterns that exclude evidence .gz files and landing media, and GIT_CACHE_STEPS. Base lines 714-717 and 788-795 previously allowed pull requests to save caches when no prior entry was restored. The head appears to tighten this so pull requests never save, which reduces cache-poisoning surface from PRs.

This file is test code only and does not affect the provider runtime, the coordinator contract or threat-model assets (A-001..A-019, TB-002..TB-013). There is one concern for integration. Restoring a cached .git and validating it with connectivity-only fsck does not verify that the checked-out tree matches the PR SHA. The actual .github/workflows/ci.yml and the action changes were not supplied, so these points remain unverified:
- that checkout still fetches and verifies the expected ref after a .git restore;
- that cache keys cannot be written by pull-request or fork contexts;
- that release-integrity or release-artifact lanes (A-011, A-017, T-054) do not consume restored .git or Swift caches in ways that could affect signed artifacts.

The workflow/action itself is required for confirmation. No regression is evidenced in this unit.
